   "format": "mcq",
   "stem": {
      "text": "Let F be the antiderivative of \\( f(x) = \\left(e^{x} + 1\\right)^{2} e^{x} \\) with \\( F(0) = 1 \\). Find the exact value of \\( F\\left(\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}\\right) \\)."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": [
         "Rational",
         59,
         3
      ]
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "Let \\( u = e^{x} + 1 \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Add",
            [
               "Exp",
               "x"
            ],
            1
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "Then \\( du = e^{x} \\, dx \\), which is exactly the factor left in the integrand, so no constant is needed.",
         "mathjson": 1
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "Since \\( F(\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}) = F(0) + \\int_{0}^{\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}} \\left(e^{x} + 1\\right)^{2} e^{x} \\, dx \\), convert the limits: \\( x = 0 \\) gives \\( u = 2 \\) and \\( x = \\ln{\\left(3 \\right)} \\) gives \\( u = 4 \\), so the integral becomes \\( \\int_{2}^{4} u^{2} \\, du \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Power",
            "u",
            2
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "An antiderivative of \\( u^{2} \\) is \\( \\frac{u^{3}}{3} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Divide",
            [
               "Power",
               "u",
               3
            ],
            3
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 5,
         "text": "The integral is \\( \\frac{64}{3} - \\left(\\frac{8}{3}\\right) = \\frac{56}{3} \\), so \\( F(\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}) = 1 + \\frac{56}{3} = \\frac{59}{3} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Rational",
            59,
            3
         ]
      }
   ],
